WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.460
Secure boot helps ensure that expected
firmware is verified before execution.

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.908
At a high level, the device starts from a
trusted or protected point,

3
00:00:07.988 --> 00:00:09.230
verifies the next stage,

4
00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.678
and continues only when the policy says
the firmware is acceptable.

5
00:00:12.758 --> 00:00:15.300
The exact implementation depends on the
platform,

6
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.448
but the design questions are consistent.

7
00:00:17.528 --> 00:00:21.429
Firmware integrity depends on a chain of
trust, protected verification keys,

8
00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:23.518
signed images, and defined boot policy.

9
00:00:23.598 --> 00:00:26.612
Developers should understand where the
trust anchor lives,

10
00:00:26.692 --> 00:00:30.238
which firmware stages are checked, how
image metadata is interpreted,

11
00:00:30.318 --> 00:00:33.332
and which configuration settings affect
startup behavior.

12
00:00:33.412 --> 00:00:37.490
This is design and review work that should
rely on supported platform mechanisms.

13
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.353
Update validation should check
authenticity, integrity, version policy,

14
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:43.915
image format, image size, target
compatibility,

15
00:00:43.995 --> 00:00:48.014
and any product-specific requirements
before the device accepts new firmware.

16
00:00:48.094 --> 00:00:50.812
A valid signature alone may not answer
every question;

17
00:00:50.892 --> 00:00:54.675
the image still needs to be appropriate
for the device and its current state.

18
00:00:54.755 --> 00:00:56.882
Older-version policy should be explicit.

19
00:00:56.962 --> 00:01:01.336
Some products use platform-supported
anti-rollback concepts to avoid unintended use

20
00:01:01.416 --> 00:01:05.198
of older firmware where that fits the
product risk and hardware capability.

21
00:01:05.278 --> 00:01:08.765
The policy should be documented so
developers, manufacturing teams,

22
00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:11.150
and support teams understand what is
allowed.

23
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.589
Failure behavior matters.

24
00:01:12.669 --> 00:01:16.747
If validation fails, metadata is damaged,
storage is full, power is interrupted,

25
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:19.014
or the new image does not pass health
checks,

26
00:01:19.094 --> 00:01:23.113
the device should avoid unsafe,
inconsistent, or permanently unusable states.

27
00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:26.384
Recovery behavior should be designed
before the field needs it.

28
00:01:26.464 --> 00:01:30.365
Secure boot and update design should rely
on platform-supported mechanisms,

29
00:01:30.445 --> 00:01:33.932
vendor-supported features, and approved
cryptographic libraries.

30
00:01:34.012 --> 00:01:37.203
Teams should avoid inventing their own
cryptographic schemes,

31
00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:39.765
boot policy formats, or secret-storage
designs.

32
00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:44.100
Use documented capabilities and then test
the success and failure paths on realistic

33
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.067
configurations.

34
00:01:45.147 --> 00:01:47.393
Review should include production settings.

35
00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:50.605
Debug state, key provisioning, boot
options, storage layout,

36
00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:52.517
signing workflow, update metadata,

37
00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:56.793
and recovery permissions can all affect
the real security properties of the device.

38
00:01:56.873 --> 00:01:59.060
Treat those settings as release artifacts.
